            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Jean Monnet</strong><br>\nJean Monnet was born on November 9, 1888, in Cognac, France, into a family of cognac merchants. Growing up, he was exposed to the business and political ties of his father, which spanned across countries. Despite having no formal university education, Monnet was well-versed in the world of finance and trade due to his family's business. His early life experiences with international trade laid the foundation for his future role in shaping European cooperation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Career Beginnings</strong><br>\nMonnet began his career by helping to manage his family's cognac business. His work took him to various countries, including the UK, where he learned English and gained insights into international banking and finance. His acumen in these fields grew as he became a representative for the family company in the United States during the First World War, which deepened his understanding of international economics and politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Role in International Affairs</strong><br>\nDuring World War I, Monnet coordinated the supply of allied forces, which improved his stature as a skilled organizer on an international level. After the war, he worked on international projects, including the League of Nations, where he attempted to implement his early ideas for European collaboration. However, the League did not succeed, leading Monnet to return to business before reengaging with politics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
