      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Vision for Europe</strong><br>\nMonnet's vision for a united Europe was conceived during the interwar period. He believed that the economic and political integration of European countries was essential for maintaining peace and stability on the continent. His ideas were considered innovative at the time and laid the groundwork for the European Coal and Steel Community, a precursor to the European Union.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II Contributions</strong><br>\nWith the outbreak of World War II, Monnet was appointed as the deputy secretary-general of the League of Nations. However, he soon resigned to assist with the British and French war efforts. He proposed the unification of British and French resources, which, though not fully realized, was a significant step towards his later work in European integration.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Monnet Plan</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, Monnet proposed the Monnet Plan, which aimed to revitalize the French economy through modernization and to prevent the spread of communism by ensuring France's economic strength. This plan was pivotal in securing U.S. financial aid through the Marshall Plan and set the scene for economic cooperation in Europe.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
