          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Founding the ECSC</strong><br>\nJean Monnet's efforts culminated in the creation of the European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC) in 1951. He was the chief architect of this organization, which was the first of a series of supranational European institutions that would eventually become the European Union. The ECSC was designed to pool coal and steel production in Europe, thereby binding the member states economically and politically.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nMonnet continued to advocate for European unity throughout his life. He founded the Action Committee for the United States of Europe, aimed at promoting his vision. His dedication to the cause of European integration earned him the title \"Father of Europe.\" Monnet's work has had a lasting impact on the shape of modern Europe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Honors</strong><br>\nJean Monnet passed away on March 16, 1979. His legacy includes the Jean Monnet Program, established to promote studies in European integration. Awards and institutions bearing his name continue to honor his contributions to the development of the European Union. Monnet's vision and actions have left an indelible mark on the course of European history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
